    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "How many natural numbers $N$ have the property that among the numbers $1, 2, \\dots, N$ exactly 30% are divisible by 3?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "0"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "1"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "2"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "3"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "infinitely many"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Compare with one third",
        "body": "Among $1, \\dots, N$ there are $\\lfloor N/3 \\rfloor$ multiples of 3, which is always more than $\\frac{N}{3} - 1$. We need it to equal $0.3N$, which must be a whole number, so $N$ is a multiple of 10: $N = 10k$, and we need $\\lfloor 10k/3 \\rfloor = 3k$.\n\nThe number $\\frac{10k}{3}$ lies between $3k$ and $3k+1$ exactly when $\\frac{k}{3} < 1$, i.e. $k = 1$ or $2$.\n\n- $N = 10$: multiples 3, 6, 9 — 3 of 10 = 30% ✓\n- $N = 20$: 6 of 20 = 30% ✓\n- $N = 30$: 10 of 30 — too many, and for larger $N$ the share gets closer to $33\\frac13\\%$.\n\nSo there are 2 such numbers.\n\n**Answer: (C) 2**"
      }
    ],
